                    Google Scholar</a>&nbsp;
                </p></li></ul><p class="c-article-references__download u-hide-print"><a data-track="click" data-track-action="download citation references" data-track-label="link" rel="nofollow" href="https://citation-needed.springer.com/v2/references/10.1007/s10750-023-05177-8?format=refman&amp;flavour=references">Download references<svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-download-medium"></use></svg></a></p></div></div></div></section></div><section data-title="Acknowledgements"><div class="c-article-section" id="Ack1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Ack1">Acknowledgements</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Ack1-content"><p>The authors thank Lyubov Lapshina and Lyudmila Udachina (South Urals Federal Research Center of Mineralogy and Geoecology of the Urals Branch of the Russian Academy of Sciences) for generous help with the analysis of lake water, Dmitry Pirogov (Ural Electrochemical Integrated Plant) for chemical analysis of Lake Tavatui sediments, Stephen Juggins (Newcastle University, UK) for providing the opportunity to work with the combined EDDI TP dataset, Vasiliy Grigor’ev (St. Petersburg State University) for consultation on interpretation of <sup>210</sup>Pb analysis results, and Elena Maslennikova for recommendations that improved the text. We are grateful to the reviewers of this paper for their valuable recommendations.</p></div></div></section><section data-title="Funding"><div class="c-article-section" id="Fun-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Fun">Funding</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Fun-content"><p>The ecological analysis, quantitative reconstructions, Lake Turgoyak sediment analysis, and lake water analysis were supported by the Russian Science Foundation (Grant No. 21-17-00071, <a href="https://rscf.ru/project/21-17-00071/">https://rscf.ru/project/21-17-00071/</a>). The diatom analysis, age-depth model, and chemical analysis of the sediments of lakes Syrytkul, Tavatui, and Ufimskoe were funded by the State Contract of South Urals Federal Research Center of Mineralogy and Geoecology of the Urals Branch of the Russian Academy of Sciences.</p></div></div></section><section aria-labelledby="author-information" data-title="Author information"><div class="c-article-section" id="author-information-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="author-information">Author information</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="author-information-content"><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="affiliations">Authors and Affiliations</h3><ol class="c-article-author-affiliation__list"><li id="Aff1"><p class="c-article-author-affiliation__address">South Urals Federal Research Center of Mineralogy and Geoecology of the Ural Branch of the Russian Academy of Sciences, Miass, Chelyabinsk Region, Russia</p><p class="c-article-author-affiliation__authors-list">Anna Maslennikova,&nbsp;Valery Udachin,&nbsp;Dmitry Artemyev,&nbsp;Ksenya Filippova,&nbsp;Vasiliy Gulakov,&nbsp;Nikolai Udachin&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Pavel Aminov</p></li><li id="Aff2"><p class="c-article-author-affiliation__address">South Ural State Humanitarian Pedagogical University, Chelyabinsk, Russia</p><p class="c-article-author-affiliation__authors-list">Vladimir Deryagin</p></li><li id="Aff3"><p class="c-article-author-affiliation__address">South Ural State University, Chelyabinsk, Russia</p><p class="c-article-author-affiliation__authors-list">Valery Udachin</p></li></ol><div class="u-js-hide u-hide-print" data-test="author-info"><span class="c-article__sub-heading">Authors</span><ol class="c-article-authors-search u-list-reset"><li id="auth-Anna-Maslennikova-Aff1"><span class="c-article-authors-search__title u-h3 